+# Running Saber on a 4× Radeon AI PRO R9700 host
+
+Status: hardware guide, 2026-11-06. This documents what a four-card R9700 host can do with Saber **today**, and what it cannot. It does not claim TP=4 support.
+
+## The host
+
+| Part | Choice | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| GPUs | 4× Radeon AI PRO R9700 32 GB (gfx1201), 2-slot blower, 300 W, 1× 12V-2x6 | Saber uses two per TP=2 server; a future 4-rank mode would use all four. |
+| Board | Gigabyte TRX50 AI TOP | 4× PCIe 5.0 x16, 4× PCIe 5.0 x4 M.2, 8 DDR5 RDIMM slots. |
+| CPU | Ryzen Threadripper PRO 9955WX (16C/32T, 128 PCIe 5.0 lanes, 8-channel) | The cheapest 9000WX part that unlocks all four x16 slots, all four M.2 slots and 8-channel memory. |
+| RAM | 2× 64 GB DDR5-5600 2Rx4 ECC RDIMM (128 GB now; 8× = 512 GB later) | The PLE table is `O_DIRECT` from NVMe, so RAM is the OS/checkpoint floor, not a PLE cache. |
+| Storage | 4× 1 TB NVMe RAID 0 (4 TB) + 1× 4 TB SATA SSD | Flash-Next needs ~70 GB of packs + a 170 GB checkpoint + a 95.4 GiB PLE table read in place; 4 TB holds both Saber and a Strata IQ3_S install. |
+| PSU | 2,200 W ATX 3.1 (Seasonic PRIME PX-2200) | Saber caps at 210 W/card, so 4×210 W is easy. The 2,200 W unit is sized for Strata’s 4×300 W mode; it is not required for Saber alone. |
+| Cooling / case | 360 mm sTR5 AIO, Fractal Meshify 2 XL | Four 2-slot blowers, 9+3 slots, 250 mm PSU, 480 mm top radiator. |
+
+Measured reference point: **Qwen3.8-27B, TP=2 on two R9700s, 229 tok/s single-stream decode, ~47 ms TTFT, 5,000+ tok/s prefill at a 210 W/card cap, 262K context** (the repo README’s number). Flash-Next is served by the same binary; no Flash-Next decode number is published.
+
+## What works today
+
+### One TP=2 server on a pair
+
+Use the existing launchers. For Flash-Next, `tools/serve_flashnext.sh` already reads `HIP_DEVICES` (default `0,1`); set it to the pair you want:
+
+```sh
+HIP_DEVICES=0,1 PORT=8080 CNAME=saber-fn-a tools/serve_flashnext.sh
+```
+
+The other pair is idle for that model. The server is OpenAI-compatible on `:8080`.
+
+For the 27B, `tools/serve_27b.sh` selects `--engine-device 0 --engine-peer-device 1`; to pin it to a pair, the container must see that pair as devices 0 and 1. The launcher does not yet forward an explicit pair variable to the container, so a one-line `-e HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=...` change is needed for pair selection. The default pair is 0,1.
+
+### Two independent TP=2 servers (two models)
+
+The engine is built for one TP=2 server at a time. The Flash-Next launcher refuses to start while another `saber-fn`/`saber-flashnext-serve` container is running, so two servers on disjoint pairs are **not supported by the launcher today**. The intended multi-model path is one server at a time; if you need two models on four cards, that needs a launcher change and a 4-card test. It still serves two models, not one model across four cards.
+
+### Not supported: TP=3 / TP=4
+
+`--tp 3/4` is refused by the CLI (`crates/saber-cli/src/main.rs`), the setup wizard only enables TP=2 (`tools/saber-setup`), and the executor/all-reduce are two-rank (`crates/saber-engine/src/device.rs`, `crates/saber-plan/src/reference.rs`). See `docs/RFC-4-RANK.md` for the design work.
+
+## Why Flash-Next cannot be dense-TP=4
+
+Flash-Next has **24 query heads and 2 KV heads** (`num_key_value_heads: 2`, GQA 12). Dense TP=4 would need 0.5 KV heads per rank; the graph’s TP divisor refuses a dimension that does not divide by `tp`. The current pack is **EP=2 (256 of 512 experts per rank) + dense TP=2**; a four-GPU version must choose EP=4 with TP=2 pairs, EP=4 with TP=1, or a pipeline split — not `tp = 4`.
+
+The 27B is different: 24 q heads and 4 KV heads divide by 4 (6 q, 1 KV; GDN 16/48 divide by 4). A 27B TP=4 is graph-legal, but the executor, all-reduce and pack are still two-rank, so it is not available today either.
+
+## Storage and the PLE table
+
+Saber’s Flash-Next pack points into AMD’s 170 GB checkpoint for the embeddings, norms, router and the per-layer-embedding table. The table is **320,001,536 rows × 160 bf16 = 95.4 GiB**, read 16 rows (5,120 bytes) per token with `O_DIRECT`. Keep the checkpoint and packs on the 4 TB NVMe array; the 4 TB SATA is the cold archive. A 2 TB array would be tight once both Saber and Strata packs are installed; 4 TB is the comfortable size.
+
+## What to measure on a 4-card host
+
+1. One TP=2 server on 0,1; record decode, TTFT, prefill, wall power, per-card VRAM.
+2. The same on 2,3; confirm the pair is symmetric.
+3. A 2×2 attempt (two servers, disjoint pairs, distinct `CNAME`/`PORT`/`SABER_SHARED`/`HIP_DEVICES`) **only** after the launcher check is relaxed; record whether both boot and whether the engine’s IPC/ports interfere.
+4. For a future 4-rank mode: token equality, 262K stream stability, per-card VRAM, 4-rank all-reduce correctness, wall power.
+
+## What this guide does not claim
+
+- It does not claim 4-rank support, TP=4, or a 4× decode number.
+- It does not claim two servers on four cards work with today’s launcher.
+- The 229 tok/s figure is the 27B dense model, not Flash-Next.
