+# RFC: four-rank Saber for a 4× R9700 host
+
+Status: proposal, 2026-11-06. This is a design note, not an implementation. It exists because a four-card R9700 host is a real configuration (`docs/4X-R9700.md`) and the current engine is TP=1/2 only.
+
+## Summary
+
+Saber’s Flash-Next path is **EP=2 over two ranks, dense TP=2**. The dense attention has **2 KV heads**, so a dense TP=4 split would need 0.5 KV heads per rank; that is not a head count. A four-card mode therefore needs a different decomposition. Two candidates:
+
+1. **EP=4 + dense TP=2 (two pairs).** 512/4 = 128 experts per rank; the dense layers stay TP=2 within each pair; the 2 KV heads are split 1 per rank inside a pair and replicated across pairs. Closest to today’s code; needs a 4-rank MoE combine.
+2. **Pipeline / layer split.** Each rank owns 16 of the 64 layers, with its own dense weights, experts and KV. This is the design that preserves 8×262K streams; it is what Strata already does.
+
+For the stated objective (one Flash-Next model, four cards, 8×262K streams), **the pipeline split is the design that meets the memory target**. EP=4+TP=2 is an intermediate that may fit 8 streams but duplicates KV across pairs and gives limited speed-up. The 27B (4 KV heads) is a separate, smaller TP=4 case.
+
+## Current constraints (code at `cebec82`)
+
+| Constraint | Where |
+|---|---|
+| Flash-Next pack = EP=2 + dense TP=2 | `crates/saber-model/src/qwen4_exp/pack_plan.rs` (`EP`, `TP` constants) |
+| Flash-Next dense TP has only One/Two | `crates/saber-model/src/qwen4_exp/layer_graph.rs` (`enum Tp`) |
+| Flash-Next has 2 KV heads | `crates/saber-model/src/qwen4_exp/config.rs` |
+| All-reduce is 2-rank | `crates/saber-plan/src/reference.rs` (`world_size == 2`) |
+| Executor refuses world != 1/2 | `crates/saber-engine/src/device.rs` |
+| CLI refuses tp != 1/2 | `crates/saber-cli/src/main.rs` |
+| Launchers hardcode TP=2 | `tools/serve_flashnext.sh`, `tools/serve_27b.sh` |
+
+The graph builder already takes a `tp` parameter and divides dimensions generically, but the executor, collectives and pack are the two-rank parts.
+
+## Option 1 — EP=4 + dense TP=2
+
+**Layout.** Four ranks in two pairs. Each rank holds:
+
+- half the dense weights (TP=2), 1.75 GB;
+- 128 routed experts (EP=4), 12.5 GB;
+- one of the two KV heads (TP=2 inside the pair), replicated across pairs: 10.55 GB for 8×262K;
+- buffers ~2 GB, head/draft ~0.8 GB.
+
+Total ≈27.6 GB/rank, ~29.1 GB on the head/vision rank. **8×262K is tight but arithmetically possible** (about 3 GB free on the main rank). It requires:
+
+- a 4-rank MoE combine (each rank computes 128 experts; the routed output is the sum across four ranks);
+- pair-local TP=2 for dense/attention, with the pair’s result replicated to the other pair;
+- a 4-rank pack: `rank0..3`, each with half-dense + 128 experts; the pack builder (`tools/pack/build_flashnext_pack.py`) must be generalized from `EP=2/TP=2`;
+- a 4-rank command/rendezvous protocol (today `saber-engine/src/tp2.rs` is a two-rank link);
+- a 4-rank all-reduce or a hierarchical 2×2 reduce (reduce within each pair, then across pairs).
+
+**Pros:** closest to the current model graph; no layer handoff; each pair can run the existing TP=2 dense path.
+**Cons:** KV is duplicated across pairs (half the per-card KV capacity is wasted); attention/dense compute is duplicated; the 8-stream fit is tight; the speed-up over one TP=2 server is limited to the expert split; a new 4-rank MoE combine is still needed.
+
+## Option 2 — pipeline / layer split
+
+**Layout.** Each rank owns 16 of the 64 layers (or another contiguous split). Each rank holds:
+
+- its layers’ dense weights: ~0.9 GB;
+- its layers’ experts: 12.5 GB;
+- its layers’ KV: 5.3 GB for 8×262K;
+- buffers ~2 GB; head/draft on the last rank ~0.8 GB.
+
+Total ≈21.5 GB/rank; **8×262K fits with ~10 GB free**. There is no KV replication and no duplicated dense compute.
+
+**Needs:**
+
+- a layer handoff per token/verify window (pipeline), with the hidden state moving rank to rank;
+- per-rank KV and per-rank expert caches (the pack is layer-sharded, not expert-parallel across all layers);
+- a scheduler that keeps the pipeline full (prefill chunks, decode windows);
+- a new pack layout and builder;
+- a 4-rank control plane.
+
+**Pros:** the only option that comfortably meets 8×262K and scales with four cards; no KV duplication.
+**Cons:** a new executor, not a TP knob; the current `tp2` command protocol and `Graph` are built around a two-rank pair. This is the largest change, but it is the design the objective needs.
+
+## Option 3 — 27B TP=4 (separate)
+
+The 27B has 24 q heads and **4 KV heads**, so TP=4 is head-legal (6 q, 1 KV; GDN 16/48 divide by 4). A 27B TP=4 could reuse a 4-rank all-reduce and a 4-rank pack without the Flash-Next MoE complications. It does not solve the Flash-Next 2-KV-head problem, but it is a smaller first step for validating a 4-rank executor. If the maintainer wants a staged path: 27B TP=4 first, then EP=4/pipeline for Flash-Next.
+
+## Suggested sequence
+
+1. **Docs (this PR):** `docs/4X-R9700.md`, the hardware envelope and the 2-card limits.
+2. **RFC (this document):** agree on Option 1 vs Option 2. The recommendation for 8×262K is Option 2.
+3. **Prototype:** a 4-rank all-reduce and a 4-rank command frame, tested on synthetic buffers before any model.
+4. **27B TP=4:** validate the 4-rank path on the dense model (4 KV heads, simpler graph).
+5. **Flash-Next:** implement the chosen decomposition (EP=4 or pipeline), generate 4-rank packs, add gates.
+6. **Launchers / setup:** a four-device selector, a 4-rank pack, and a `serve_flashnext_4x.sh`.
+
+## Acceptance gates
+
+- token equality vs the TP=2 reference on a fixed prompt set;
+- 262K context with 8 concurrent streams, no drops, no VRAM growth over 24 h;
+- per-card VRAM ≤30 GB, wall power ≤1,600 W (4×210 W cap);
+- 4-rank all-reduce correctness (sum and bit-exact where required);
+- pack rank/TP header checks, cold-start time, and a 4-card thermal soak;
+- a documented fallback to the TP=2 server if a rank fails.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- This RFC does not promise a date or a speed-up.
+- It does not change the hardware requirement: 4× R9700 32 GB, 128 GB host RAM, a 4 TB NVMe array and a 2,200 W PSU are already the right envelope (`docs/4X-R9700.md`).
+- It does not claim the 229 tok/s 27B number transfers to Flash-Next.
